!****************************************************************
!This test consists of a gas with an initial population of dust,
! both silicon and carbon based, with 10 bins each.
!The dust-to-gas mass ratio is 1e-2 and the dust distribution
! follows an MRN power-law. (legacy tests/dust)
!****************************************************************

KROME-X notes
- C++ driver (test.cpp): the population is a DustBins (10 bins per type,
  MRN 5e-7 - 2.5e-5 cm, the legacy krome_init_dust_distribution defaults);
  dust.evolve = ["GROWTH", "EVAP"] (legacy -dustOptions) makes the grain radii
  solver state. Tdust = Tgas as in legacy (dust.evolve_Td with thermal
  evolution off holds the given Td); d2g = 1e-2 (the legacy header and
  README said 1e-5, the driver used 1e-2).
- outputs as legacy: gas.dat (Tgas, gas-phase C and Si mass densities),
  dust.dat (dust type 0 carbon / 1 silicon, Tgas, dust mass density per
  bin, one block per bin), mass_check.dat (Tgas, total C + Si mass: constant,
  the exchange conserves the key elements exactly). The dust mass uses
  each type's bulk density (2.25 / 3.13 g cm^-3; legacy wrote 2.2 for
  both). Plot: python3 plot.py.
- KROME-X vs legacy (the same run, gas-phase mass densities [g cm^-3]):

      Tgas [K]   C KROME-X      C legacy   Si KROME-X     Si legacy
      10         1.91e-21  1.72e-21   4.65e-21  4.62e-21
      327        1.89e-22  3.73e-25   4.01e-21  9.70e-22
      1322       1.91e-21  1.72e-21   6.47e-21  4.62e-21
      2656       1.70e-20  9.66e-21   6.47e-21  6.48e-21
      1e4        1.70e-20  1.69e-20   6.47e-21  6.48e-21

  The same shape (depletion at intermediate Tgas, full return when the
  grains evaporate; the same final gas values). The differences are the
  KROME-X fixes of the legacy dust evolution:
  - growth: legacy used the proton's thermal speed for the accreting
    atom, 3.5x (C) and 5.3x (Si) too fast: KROME-X depletes less;
  - evaporation: KROME-X removes atomic layers at the Polanyi-Wigner rate,
    size independent (Grassi et al. 2017 sec. 2.3), with E0 = 7.2 eV
    (carbon, Lenzuni et al. 1995) and 4.66 eV (silicate, Nozawa et al.
    2006); legacy removed one atom per grain (a rate ~1/a^2) with Evans's
    7.66 / 5.22 eV. Silicates evaporate by ~1300 K, carbon by ~2600 K.
- output files (legacy name): gas.dat (fort.66), dust.dat (fort.77), mass_check.dat (fort.88).
